  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "The Topeka NAACP deliberately organized a class action with 13 parents on behalf of 20 children; Oliver Brown was recruited to join and named because he was the only man in the group, and the Supreme Court decided Kansas's case together with cases from South Carolina, Virginia, Delaware and the District of Columbia involving nearly 200 people.",
   "uso:myth": "The case began when Oliver Brown alone sued after his daughter Linda was turned away from a nearby white school and forced to walk far to a Black school.",
   "uso:why": "Reducing Brown to one family's story hides the years of organized legal strategy and the many students, parents, teachers and lawyers, in the South and beyond it, who took risks to bring the cases."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why did the NAACP's lawyers value the Kansas case even though Topeka's Black and white schools were roughly comparable in buildings and resources?",
   "uso:answer": "Because the facilities were substantially comparable, the Kansas case forced the Court to decide whether segregation itself was unconstitutional rather than merely ordering states to equalize unequal schools, and it showed that school segregation was a national issue and not only a Southern one.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:brown-v-board-of-education"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why was the District of Columbia case, Bolling v. Sharpe, decided separately from the four state cases?",
   "uso:answer": "The Fourteenth Amendment's Equal Protection Clause restrains the states, not the federal government, so segregation in the federal capital had to be challenged under the Fifth Amendment instead.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:brown-v-board-of-education"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Court's approach to remedies allow segregation to continue for years after 1954?",
   "uso:answer": "The 1954 ruling did not say how or when schools must desegregate; Brown II in 1955 left it to federal district courts to proceed \"with all deliberate speed,\" and opponents used that vague standard to delay, admit only token numbers of Black students, pass new segregation laws, or, as in Prince Edward County, close public schools from 1959 to 1964.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:brown-v-board-of-education"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Students led by 16-year-old Barbara Rose Johns walk out of the overcrowded Robert Russa Moton High School in Farmville, Virginia.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1951-04-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Attorneys ask the federal court to reopen the Topeka case to test whether segregation's effects remained (Brown III).",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:gYear",
    "@value": "1979"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/2",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "NAACP lawyers file Davis v. County School Board of Prince Edward County, later joined to Brown.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1951-05-23"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/3",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Supreme Court hears the first arguments in the consolidated school cases.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1952-12-09"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/4",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Reargument begins on questions the Court posed, including the history of the Fourteenth Amendment and possible remedies.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1953-12-07"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/5",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Court rules unanimously that segregated public schools violate the Equal Protection Clause; Bolling v. Sharpe is decided the same day.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1954-05-17"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/6",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "In Brown II the Court orders desegregation to proceed with all deliberate speed under federal district courts.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1955-05-31"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/7",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "The Southern Manifesto denouncing the decision is introduced in Congress.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1956-03-12"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/8",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Prince Edward County, Virginia, cuts off public school funding rather than desegregate.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1959-06-26"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/brown-v-board-of-education/timeline/9",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Prince Edward County's public schools reopen after the Supreme Court's Griffin decision.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1964-09-08"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/misconception",
   "@type": "uso:Misconception",
   "uso:fact": "Section 2 penalized states only for denying the vote to male citizens, and in Minor v. Happersett (1875) the Supreme Court held that citizenship did not carry the right to vote; women won a constitutional guarantee only with the Nineteenth Amendment in 1920.",
   "uso:myth": "By making women citizens, the Fourteenth Amendment gave them the right to vote.",
   "uso:why": "Assuming citizenship and voting rights always went together hides how long women, and Native Americans after the 1924 citizenship act, were citizens who could still be kept from the polls."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/review/1",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "What problems was the amendment meant to solve after the Thirteenth Amendment ended slavery?",
   "uso:answer": "Southern states were passing Black Codes to limit freed people's rights while white southerners terrorized them, and Dred Scott's rule still denied Black Americans citizenship; the amendment made all persons born or naturalized in the country citizens, barred states from denying due process or equal protection, and gave Congress power to enforce those guarantees.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:fourteenth-amendment"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/review/2",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "How did the Slaughter-House Cases change what the amendment meant in practice?",
   "uso:answer": "The Court held that the Privileges or Immunities Clause protected only rights of national citizenship, leaving most rights to the states and turning the clause into a practical nullity, so later protection of Bill of Rights guarantees against the states came instead through the Due Process Clause, case by case.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:fourteenth-amendment"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/review/3",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Why were former Confederate states required to ratify the amendment?",
   "uso:answer": "White southerners rejected it almost unanimously in 1866 and 1867; after Northern voters backed Republicans in the 1866 elections, Congress made ratification a condition for restoring a state's representation, and Southern ratifications in 1868 completed the three-fourths.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:fourteenth-amendment"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/review/4",
   "@type": "uso:ReviewQuestion",
   "rdfs:label": "Who was left out of the citizenship clause at first, and how did that change?",
   "uso:answer": "Native people under tribal rather than U.S. authority were excluded, and Elk v. Wilkins (1884) held that Native Americans were not U.S. citizens even after leaving their nations; Congress made all Native Americans citizens only with the Indian Citizenship Act of 1924.",
   "uso:questionFor": {
    "@id": "us:fourteenth-amendment"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/timeline/1",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Congress creates the Joint Committee of Fifteen on Reconstruction.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1865-12-13"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/timeline/10",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Minor v. Happersett holds that citizenship does not carry a right to vote.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1875-03-29"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/timeline/11",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "Elk v. Wilkins holds that Native Americans who had left their nations were still not U.S. citizens.",
   "uso:date": {
    "@type": "xsd:date",
    "@value": "1884-11-03"
   }
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/fourteenth-amendment/timeline/12",
   "@type": "uso:TimelineEntry",
   "rdfs:label": "United States v. Wong Kim Ark applies birthright citizenship to a U.S.-born son of Chinese parents.",
   "uso:date": {
